<h2>Why I started</h2>
<p>I was looking for something I could build once and keep selling. A Chrome extension costs only $5 to register. I also saw the stat that 57% of extensions never earn a cent. I went in knowing that.</p>
<p>I had the AI dig through 20 Google autocomplete keywords and the top extensions in the store. What it found was a leader with lots of users and bad reviews. "Save as PDF" had 300,000 users, a 3.2 rating, and no update in over a year. The low-star reviews had two complaints: pages behind a login can't be saved, and the free version only gives you a PDF that says "sign up".</p>

<h2>What one day produced</h2>
<p>Those two complaints became the feature list. It uses the PDF engine already inside Chrome, so it saves exactly what's on screen without sending anything to a server. Logged-in pages work. I added a clean mode that hides menus, ads and cookie banners, plus paper size and margins.</p>
<p>Screenshots, store images and the permission justifications were done on day one. The next day I built Pro: headers and footers, a long page as one sheet, and saving every tab in the window. It unlocks with a Gumroad license key, for $9.99.</p>

<h2>Where it got stuck</h2>
<p>Not in building, in registering. Email verification failed and blocked submission, and seller verification said "daily verification limit exceeded". I shipped the free version as a non-trader first, and it went live five days later.</p>
<p>Payments were another wall. Stripe doesn't accept Korean accounts, so I went with Gumroad, which only pays out to Korea once $100 has built up. When I submitted Pro, organization verification failed because I have no D&B number. I couldn't find a free way for a Korean business to look one up. Pro is still in review, and it has sold zero copies.</p>

<h2>What a human had to do</h2>
<p>Paying the $5 fee, email verification, retrying seller verification, signing up for Gumroad and the ID check, and deciding whether my real name shows as the seller. Again, every spot that involved money or my name needed a person.</p>

<h2>What I learned</h2>
<p>The work took 1 hour. The extension I built in a day took five days to go live, and I'm still not set up to get paid. Other people's low-star reviews were a free product spec. And selling abroad from Korea takes longer in verification and payments than in building.</p>
